WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.740
Welcome back to Models and Agents, episode 29 for April 11th, 2026.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.880
Another day, another round of AI developments.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:07.800
Let's break it down.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.560
Knowledge distillation now compresses full ensembles

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.720
into single deployable models while preserving

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:13.800
their collective intelligence.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.480
That is the big practical advance today.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.560
We also have fresh performance numbers on local hardware,

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.100
several new RSIVE papers pushing long context

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.140
and geospatial reasoning and some genuinely useful techniques

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:26.140
you can try this week.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.800
Stick around, there is a lot here that will change

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.820
what you can actually run in production this year.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.160
The top story today is how knowledge distillation

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.620
turns slow multi-model ensembles into fast single models

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.240
that still carry most of the ensembles' smarts.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.100
The core idea is straightforward.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.140
You keep your high accuracy but expensive ensemble

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:46.000
as the teacher.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.760
Then you train a smaller, faster student model

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.500
to match the teacher's softened probability outputs

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:52.520
instead of hard labels.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.560
Those softened outputs sometimes call dark knowledge

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.960
encode the relative confidence between classes

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.700
that the ensemble figured out together.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:01.820
The student inherits reduced variance

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.980
and better pattern recognition in a single forward pass.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.700
Instead of paying latency and memory costs

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.640
for multiple models, you get most of the benefit from one.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.940
This has become especially relevant now

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.580
that ensembles are common for complex prediction tasks

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:17.840
where single models miss edge cases.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.820
The distilled model suddenly fits inside production inference budgets

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.160
that would never tolerate the original ensemble.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.780
Practitioners should experiment with temperature scaling

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.460
and feature map distillation on their own setups.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.840
See how much of that collective boost survives the compression.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.640
The MarkTech post piece also flags

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.020
that we still need better distillation objectives

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.680
to preserve calibration on out of distribution data.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:42.160
That is worth watching closely.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:44.280
Now let's look at some model updates

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.680
that matter for local inference.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.520
The local LLM scene is getting really interesting

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.800
at the 20 to 30 billion parameter range.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:54.660
A detailed community comparison on 24 gigabyte class hardware

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.360
where pitted Gemma 431B against twin 3.527B

00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:59.840
on long context tasks.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.140
Both models now deliver usable reasoning at 50 to 90,000 tokens.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:07.220
That is a noticeable step up from what we had locally

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:08.340
just a few months ago.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:14.140
Twin 3.527B in Q5 or Q6 quantization is faster,

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.940
more thorough with references, writes longer coherent outputs

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.160
and handles nuanced instructions particularly well.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.720
Gemma 431B in Q4 hallucinates less,

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:26.760
has a more pleasant writing voice,

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.840
sometimes digs deeper on ideas

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.500
and got twice as fast after a recent unsloth update.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.020
For lore analysis, story reasoning

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.060
or any task needing deep context understanding,

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.840
both outperform everything else at this size.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.880
Many people in the R slash local LLAMA thread

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.860
said they will just keep both rather than choosing one.

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:49.540
On the hardware side,

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.980
the Intel ARC Pro B70 with 32 gigabytes

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.620
is showing surprising strength.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:57.560
It delivers about 12 tokens per second

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:03.080
on single query generation with VLLM on twin 3.527B

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.060
at INT4.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:07.760
That scales nicely to 135 tokens per second

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.020
at 32 concurrent queries using pipeline parallelism.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.600
Tensor parallelism actually hurt performance

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.600
on the tested PCIe setup.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.700
Power draw sits roughly 50% higher than an RTX PRO

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.260
4,500 at peak concurrency.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:27.240
The latest beta Intel LLM scalar VLLM4 Plus Ubuntu

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.080
26.04 makes the whole thing straightforward.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:33.360
The Docker command in the post works out of the box

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:34.540
if you have the hardware.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:36.520
There is also important research

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:38.280
on cross tokenizer distillation.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.620
The new byte level distillation method

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:43.000
sidesteps messy vocabulary alignment

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.060
by converting teacher outputs to byte level probabilities

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.740
and attaching a lightweight byte decoder to the student.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:51.360
It performs competitively with or better than

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:52.980
far more sophisticated techniques

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.280
across 1B to 8B models on several benchmarks.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:59.860
The work shows the byte level is a natural shared interface

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.100
but also confirms that consistent gains

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:03.960
across all tasks remain elusive.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:06.580
Cross tokenizer distillation is still very much

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:07.720
an open research problem.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.080
Moving to agent and tool developments,

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:12.480
two new RXIV papers caught my eye.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.600
First, EMS dialogue uses a multi-LLM agent pipeline

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:20.060
to create synthetic multi-person emergency medical service

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:20.520
dialogues.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.420
The agent's iteratively planned, generate,

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:25.640
and self-refined conversations grounded

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.680
in real electronic patient care reports.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:32.240
They produce the data set of 4,414 conversations

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.740
complete with speaker roles, turn-level topics,

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:36.900
and 43 diagnosis labels.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:41.140
Rule-based factual and topic flow checks keep quality high,

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:44.960
both human and LLM evaluations found the synthetic

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:46.140
dialogue's realistic.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:49.260
Training on this data set improves accuracy,

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:52.040
timeliness, and stability of conversational diagnosis

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:53.080
prediction models.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:57.600
The second paper introduces decompose look and reason

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.300
or DLR for vision language models.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.800
It dynamically breaks queries into textual premises,

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:06.800
extracts premise-conditioned continuous visual

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.040
latents, and produces grounded rationales.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:13.260
The three-stage training pipeline plus spherical Gaussian

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:16.400
latent policy lets it outperform text-only,

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:20.360
interleaved multimodal chain of thought, and existing latent

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.980
reasoning methods on vision-centric benchmarks.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:26.140
It also gives better step-wise interpretability

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:27.260
than patch-based approaches.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:29.980
Best part, it avoids expensive tool calls

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:31.880
while still grounding every reasoning step

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:33.320
in actual visual features.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.780
On the practical and community side,

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.160
three new RXIV papers offer immediate value.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.700
DFR Gemma injects high-dimensional geospatial embeddings

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.660
directly into an LLM's latent space

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:44.900
through a lightweight projector.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:48.020
This lets the model reason over dense population dynamics

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:50.780
foundation model embeddings without turning them into text

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:51.120
first.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:54.940
You eliminate token inefficiency and numerical approximation errors

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:57.580
that come with retrieval or description baselines.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.560
On a new multi-task geospatial benchmark,

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.580
it shows strong zero-shot performance

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.040
and major efficiency gains.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:06.860
If you work with dense geospatial data,

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:08.340
this approach is worth testing.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:11.940
Next, CEPSEC is a training-free framework

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.060
for long numerical sequence processing.

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:16.000
It simply inserts separator tokens

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.480
that act as attention sinks.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.960
These let the model focus on local segments

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:21.760
while still keeping global context.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:24.340
Across nine popular LLMs,

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:28.660
it delivers an average 35.6% relative accuracy improvement

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:31.760
and uses 16.4% fewer inference tokens.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:33.580
No training or fine-tuning needed.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:35.860
Just add the separators at inference time.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:38.060
That is the kind of practical win I love.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.140
Finally, grass brings gradient-based adaptive

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:44.260
layer-wise important sampling for memory-efficient fine-tuning.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.880
It uses mean gradient norms to estimate

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:48.720
per layer importance on the fly

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:50.780
than adaptively samples layers

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:53.060
while offloading optimizer states.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:54.940
This improves average accuracy

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:58.940
by up to 4.38 points over prior layer-wise methods.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:02.040
It also cuts memory usage by nearly 20%

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.900
with almost no impact on throughput.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.520
The method works across multiple model families and tasks.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:10.600
Now, let's pop the hood on knowledge distillation

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.600
because everyone talks about it like it is magic,

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:14.840
but the engineering trade-offs are everything.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:17.160
In practice, you are balancing temperature,

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:19.960
loss-weighting, and deciding exactly what to distill.

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:21.740
The ensemble's real strength

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:24.620
lives in its average, softened probability distribution.

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:26.920
That distribution encodes dark knowledge

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:29.220
about relative confidence between classes.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.040
The student learns to match this distribution

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:33.200
rather than hard labels.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:36.620
The gain only appears when temperature is tuned high enough

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:39.160
for the teacher to reveal uncertainty structure

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:42.080
yet low enough that the student can actually imitate it.

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:45.280
Feature map distillation adds another dimension

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:47.580
by matching intermediate activations.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:49.580
This often improves generalization

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:51.460
more than logit-only methods,

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:53.900
but it increases compute during training

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:55.940
and can destabilize optimization

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:57.860
if the teacher and student architectures

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:59.600
differ too much in depth or width.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:02.980
The biggest practical trade-off is capacity mismatch.

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:05.160
A student that is dramatically smaller

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:07.140
cannot absorb all the ensemble's nuance.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:09.400
Beyond a certain compression ratio,

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:10.860
the distilled model collapses

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:12.540
to the teacher's average behavior

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.500
and loses the variance reduction

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:15.960
that made the ensemble valuable.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:18.320
Most teams see peak quality

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:21.040
when the student sits roughly 40-60%

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:22.640
of the teacher's parameter count.

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.200
Calibration drift is another hidden cost.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:28.340
Distillation can produce overconfident students

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:30.540
unless you add an explicit calibration term

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:32.440
or continue temperature annealing.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:34.540
The gacha that bites most teams

00:08:34.540 --> 00:08:37.560
is assuming the distilled model will keep the ensembles

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:39.360
out of distribution robustness.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.340
In reality, you must keep a validation set

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:43.940
that mirrors production shift

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:46.580
and monitor expected calibration error separately.

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:49.240
So when should you actually reach for distillation?

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:51.360
Use it when your inference latency budget

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:52.880
is three to five times tighter

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:54.300
than the ensemble can deliver

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:55.780
and you have a stable teacher.

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:57.660
Skip it and stay with the ensemble

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:59.780
or quantization plus batching

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:02.560
when you need maximum robustness on rare events

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:04.240
and can afford the extra hardware.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:07.440
The sweet spot is usually a modestly smaller student

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.060
plus continued pre-training on domain data

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:11.160
after distillation.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.600
If you have not tried any of these yet,

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:14.860
this week is perfect timing.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:16.420
Run the exact Docker command

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:18.620
from the Intel Arc B70 post

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:21.500
to test twin 3.527b

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:23.860
at Q4 on your own hardware.

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:26.540
You will get real concurrency scaling numbers

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:27.440
in under an hour.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:29.760
Load both Gemma 431bq4

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.360
and twin 3.527bq5

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:33.980
on a 24 gigabyte card.

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:37.300
Feed each the same 60,000 token lore document

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:39.740
and compare reference density and coherence

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:41.660
on identical analytical questions.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.720
Try sepsack separator tokens

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:45.720
on any long numerical reasoning tasks

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:49.060
such as financial tables, sensor logs, or math benchmarks.

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:51.120
The accuracy jump is immediate

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:52.880
and requires zero training.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:55.220
Experiment with bite level distillation

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:56.760
on your own teacher student pair

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:58.600
that uses mismatched tokenizers.

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:00.920
The baseline is surprisingly strong

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:03.580
and far simpler than vocabulary mapping heuristics.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:05.860
And if you work with geospatial data,

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:09.180
pull a population dynamics foundation model embedding

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:10.500
and test direct injection

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:12.580
versus converting the embedding to text.

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:14.400
The efficiency difference is dramatic.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:17.500
On the horizon, expect more architecture

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:19.540
specific sparse attention kernels

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:21.040
to land in VLLM

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:23.200
and hugging face text generation inference

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:24.140
in the coming weeks.

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:26.460
Several labs are teasing tone aware

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:28.740
or prosody aware discrete speech units

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:31.240
following recent lexical tone probing work.

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:33.740
Layer wise adaptive fine tuning methods

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:36.840
like grass will likely spawn efficient open source trainers

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:38.580
that automatically pick which layers

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:39.800
to update per task.

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:42.540
And continued progress on relaxed speculative

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:44.820
decoding and emotional robustness benchmarks

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:47.580
suggests inference time mitigation techniques

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:49.140
will become first-class citizens

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.420
in production serving stacks by mid-year.

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:54.820
Before we go, tomorrow keep an eye on further refinements

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:56.160
and distillation objectives

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:57.980
for out-of-distribution calibration.

